Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 103, "THE KEEPER"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode. This is the episode where the audience learns that Seraphine is Eryndor; Auren and Lyra do not (Season 1 bible). Canon adjustments, made so it holds with the approved film: (1) the bible places the flashback "the morning after the Sundering", but the film prologue stages the stunning of Malachar in the Loom chamber immediately after the Sundering, so this script follows the film; (2) the seven Guardians are the film's seven: Malachar, Ithren, Eryndor, Orsolya, Maevar, Tolun, Sabeth; (3) the Citadel statue of Ithren with Auren's face is a film reveal and is deliberately NOT shown here; (4) Seraphine and Malachar do not meet (Continuity Lock 2); Torin has no face yet (Lock 6); the exact phrase "Hold the other end" is held for episode 7. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. A PROVINCIAL RAILWAY STATION — PLATFORM — EARLY MORNING

A grey platform under a sodium lamp, the first train not yet due. Pigeons. A tannoy that crackles between announcements.

A MAN in a good grey coat stands at the far end with a paper cup of coffee, his back to us, forties, perfectly still. We see his hands and the back of his head. We never see his face.

The tannoy clears its throat. In the gap before the announcement, beneath the crackle, a single low note rises: steady, dry, patient.

The man's hand tightens on the cup. The coffee trembles in a ring. He does not turn. He says, to himself, almost as a courtesy:

MAN
Not yet.

The note holds a moment longer, as if it had heard him. Then the tannoy announces a delay, and the man walks, unhurried, toward the lavatories at the end of the platform.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE GUARDIAN CITADEL — THE RECORDS ROOM — NIGHT

A round chamber of pale stone, the walls lined floor to ceiling with narrow alcoves, each holding a smooth grey tablet the size of a hand. The blue lamps burn low and, as she enters, brighten, as if recognising a footstep.

SERAPHINE comes in slowly, her bookseller's cardigan out of place among the ancient stone. She puts one hand on the doorframe and stays there a moment, the way a person does returning to a house after a long absence.

A voice, from everywhere and nowhere, precise, dry, unhurried, tender in a way that has been rehearsed for centuries:

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Keeper Eryndor.

She closes her eyes.

SERAPHINE
(quietly)
That is not what I am called now.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Ninety years. I have counted each.

SERAPHINE
I know you have.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
It is a short time, for me. I have counted it anyway.

She crosses to the centre of the room. A slow ring of blue light blooms in the floor, and seven small lamps rise from it, each a different colour, hanging in the air.

Seraphine stands among them, and for a moment her face is the face of someone very old, and someone very young.

SERAPHINE
Show me the seven.

The seven lights turn. One by one, each draws a tablet from its alcove and holds it open in the air before her: images, handwriting, a map, a name.

INSERT — A TABLET: a woman of fifty in a plain travelling coat, steady-eyed: ITHREN. Beneath her name, a short line: HOLDS MEMORY. LAST SEEN: THE SEA ROAD.

INSERT — ANOTHER: a man with kind hands and an engineer's patient eyes: MALACHAR. HOLDS TIME. And beneath it, in a different hand, added later: LOST.

Seraphine's hand rises almost to touch the second tablet, then withdraws.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
A reader has been born in the line.

SERAPHINE
(very still)
Yes.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
He hears it, and the line has not been tended, and no one has taught him what he hears. In a short time he will either be found or find.

SERAPHINE
Found by whom?

THE LAMP (V.O.)
I do not say what I do not know.

She turns slowly in the ring of lights, looking at none of them.

SERAPHINE
He stood on a chair in my shop when he was nine and tried to reach a book I had put too high. He said, "If I can't get it down, who can?" And I thought: here it is. The line, at last, after a hundred generations.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
And you have not approached him.

SERAPHINE
(low)
Every time we have reached for the line, we have hurt it.

A silence. The lamps turn.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
His mother reached. I watched what that cost. I will not be the next hand that closes round him.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Then you will watch.

SERAPHINE
(a faint, terrible smile)
It is what I am best at.

The lights drift back to their alcoves. The tablets fold away. Only one stays hanging, and she does not look at it. In the dim, the amber word LOST glows steadily on Malachar's name.

ACT TWO

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — THE SUNDERING'S AFTERMATH (FLASHBACK)

Smoke and silence. The Loom is dead, its seven rings frozen at odd angles. On the floor, the cracked throne; beside it, a body aged to bone in a robe.

The seven Guardians stand in a ring, each holding a stone. They are older than they were an hour ago: grey in their hair, a hollowness in their faces.

We are close on ERYNDOR, who looks older than all of them, and who is the only one who is looking not at the stones but at Malachar. He is standing apart, an amber stone held in both hands, eyes dry.

ITHREN
(to the ring)
They must never be together again. One hand will always want the whole.

MALACHAR
(quiet)
I won't.

We stay on Eryndor's face as the exchange goes on behind it, half heard. We hear Malachar run. We hear a pulse of grey light, like winter water. We hear a body fall.

When she moves, it is already done. Ithren kneels, trembling, with her hand on his chest.

ITHREN
(whisper)
Forgive me.

Eryndor kneels at his head. She lifts it, with infinite care, into her lap. His eyes are closed. His hair is grey at the temples now, as if the hour had cost him ten years.

The amber stone stays in his fist; nobody can open it.

ERYNDOR
(to Ithren, in a voice stripped of everything)
He will sleep. I do not know for how long.

A long moment. Eryndor looks down at the face in her hands and, very quietly, with no one near enough to hear it:

ERYNDOR (CONT'D)
(to him)
When you wake, I will tell you everything. I give you my word.

The ring of Guardians breaks and disperses. Six paths. One body, carried.

INT. A LAVATORY AT A PROVINCIAL RAILWAY STATION — DAY

A narrow white-tiled room, flickering strip-light, a cracked mirror. The man in the grey coat stands at the sink with the tap running, a phone at his ear. We see him in the mirror only from the neck down.

MAN
(low, courteous, unhurried)
It's me. She said yes to the letter.

A pause on the line. A voice we cannot hear, and the man listens with his head slightly bowed, the way one listens to someone one respects very much and does not entirely trust.

MAN (CONT'D)
No. I have not told her what I am. I told her what I advise. That's different.

Another pause.

MAN (CONT'D)
(a small, rueful breath)
You asked me to be careful. I am being careful. She'll want to see the site. When she does, I will be there. I'll not say more by phone.

He listens. Then, quite gently:

MAN (CONT'D)
(a name, for the first time)
Malachar. The signal came again this morning. On the platform. Did you hear it?

A very long silence on the line. The man turns off the tap. He looks, for the first time, directly at the mirror, and we see only the side of his face, a profile in shadow.

MAN (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Then we've begun.

He ends the call. The strip-light flickers, once. He goes out.

INT. UNIVERSITY OF HALDEN — LYRA'S OFFICE — DAY

Lyra at her desk with a thick envelope on the blotter: DRAVEN RISK ADVISORY, a crisp contract and, folded inside it, a single hand-written compliment slip: "I look forward to seeing the ridge. T. D."

HOLLIS
(reading over her shoulder)
Twelve weeks. Equipment. Security cover.

LYRA
(already signing)
"Security cover" for a dig.

HOLLIS
It's a very polite man.

LYRA
It's a very polite man, in an expensive coat, whom I have never seen, who seems to know where I will dig next.

HOLLIS
(beat)
Then why are you signing?

LYRA
(looks up; honest)
Because I'm tired of asking permission to find out whether I'm right.

She signs. Hollis says nothing. Outside the window, rain.

ACT THREE

EXT. A HILLSIDE ABOVE A GREY SEA — DAY

A bare, windy hill. At its crown, a low flat stone, the size of a bed, set into the turf with a ring of lichen-covered markers at its edges. There is nothing else here. No path leads up to it. The grass lies flat in the wind.

Seraphine climbs the hill alone in a long coat over the bookseller's cardigan, a small bunch of cut grass in one hand. She has not been here in a century. We know that from the way she holds her shoulders.

She reaches the stone. She stands over it, looking down at the place where a body once lay.

SERAPHINE
(to the stone)
I told you I would tell you the truth when you woke.

The wind. A gull, a long way out.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
You woke. And I was not there.

She kneels, lays the grass at its foot. Her hand rests flat on the cold stone a long moment. Then she opens her eyes, and stops.

On the stone, at its far corner, held down by a small pebble against the wind, is a paper bird.

She does not move. She looks at it as at a snake.

It is a very small, very clean paper bird, folded by a patient hand, crooked at one wing.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
He has been here.

She does not touch it. She looks, very slowly, at every horizon, at the empty hill, the white sea, the wind-flattened grass. Nothing. No footprint. No person.

She leans, lifts the pebble, and unfolds the paper bird with two careful fingers.

Inside, in a hand she knows, in no language she can read, a single line of strange, looping marks. Not Aurelian. Not any alphabet she has seen in four thousand years.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
What have you learned?

On the paper, the marks glimmer very faintly, like frost, and the low note, from nowhere and everywhere, rises once under the hill and holds.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH, PORT VESSA — NIGHT

The shop, shut for the night. Seraphine sits at the counter with the folded paper bird on a saucer before her and the green book beside it. She has been looking at it for an hour.

Footsteps outside. A pause at the glass. A young man in a museum lanyard, hair still wet from the rain, stops at the window and looks at the shelves without knowing why. It is AUREN.

He puts a hand to the glass. Behind it, in the half-light, the bookseller does not look up.

Auren stands there a long moment. Then he walks on.

Seraphine, at her counter, closes her eyes and does not move until the footsteps are gone.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 103 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
